// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/t27f/petal_isa_viz.t27 -- petal 0 (A ISA), the interactive visualization of one TRI-27 instruction
; word. Refs epic t27#12173 (interactive visualizations across the petals); plan row 0 of
; specs/t27f/petal_viz_plan.t27; drawn through the shared contract specs/t27f/viz_contract.t27.
; --
; What it answers: "Which bits of this instruction word mean what, and does it decode back to the same
; word?" The reader sees the 32 bits of a t27a word as a matrix, the assembler text the disassembler
; prints for it, and an inspector for one field. Hovering a bit lights its field in the text and in the
; inspector; selecting a field in the text lights its bits in the matrix. The reader flips one bit and
; watches the listing, the field values and the round trip be recomputed.
; --
; Source of truth: specs/isa/t27a.t27 (the assembler and disassembler) over specs/isa/ternary_encoding.t27
; (the field table). Every fixture word is the output of the source's own assemble; every listing
; character is the source's own listing_char; whether a word is an instruction is the source's own
; is_canonical, and the round trip is the source's own reassemble. This file adds no encoding table: its
; layout is computed from the source's shift constants, and the tests check that layout against the
; source's own readers (t27a_src1, t27a_src2, t27a_imm, v3_of, dst_of, opcode_of) and its encoder
; t27a_encode, for all 47 opcodes and for every one-bit flip of the fixtures.
; --
; Truth. The petal opens with VIZ_TRUTH_VERIFIED: the shown words are fixtures the source pins. A bit
; flip leaves that data, so the badge drops to VIZ_TRUTH_DEMO (viz_may_claim is false) until the word is
; the fixture again. A flipped word still decodes and still round trips; what is lost is the claim that
; the source pins it. A view that is not READY (empty, error, loading) shows UNKNOWN.
; --
; Layers of this spec:
;   L1 state       PavState = one VizState (the contract's) + the fixture word and the shown word;
;                  pav_apply forwards every contract event to the one reducer viz_apply and adds ONE
;                  petal event, the bit flip.
;   L2 entities    field f = viz_ref(NODE, f) for the six field slots (opcode, dst, src1, src2, v3, imm),
;                  also the focus order; matrix bit b = viz_ref(CELL, b). A cell maps to its field.
;   L3 layout      which field owns which bit, per opcode form; the owned bits; stray bits.
;   L4 matrix      32 cells in 4 rows of 8, most significant bit first: row 3 is the opcode byte.
;   L5 source      the listing, one character per position, each mapped back to its field.
;   L6 inspector   everything about the field under the pointer (else the selected one).
;   L7 badge       truth and lifecycle; the status of the word (instruction, undefined opcode, register
;                  out of range, stray bits).
;   L8 host        pav_host: nested VizHost slots for the badge, matrix, source and inspector.
; --
; Integer only. Pixel geometry is in frame coordinates (the origin is the frame's top-left corner), so a
; hit test and pav_host agree without an offset.
; --
; Not claimed: no host draws this petal yet. Wiring it into the glass window is the next child issue of
; #12173. The petal has one step and no stepper, and its choice of fixture (PARAM_SET) has no drawn
; control, because the plan row has neither rep.
; ASCII only (L3), English only (LANG-EN).
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

